# Apps mapping the codec's buffers, and the pattern behind every memfd rule on
# this device.
#
#     48x scontext=u:r:untrusted_app:s0:c72,c256,c512,c768
#         tcontext=u:object_r:tmpfs:s0 tclass=file
#     20x scontext=u:r:platform_app:s0:c512,c768
#         tcontext=u:object_r:tmpfs:s0 tclass=file
#
# These are Codec2 buffers. mediaswcodec is hal_codec2 (hal_server_domain in
# private/mediaswcodec.te) and declares no tmpfs type of its own, so its memfds
# keep the generic tmpfs label rather than a mediaswcodec_tmpfs one.
#
# private/app.te states the rule this completes, in a block it introduces as
# "Read tmpfs/memfd types from these processes":
#
#     allow appdomain audioserver_tmpfs:file    { getattr map read write };
#     allow appdomain audioserver:memfd_file    { getattr map read write };
#     allow appdomain system_server_tmpfs:file  { getattr map read write };
#     allow appdomain system_server:memfd_file  { getattr map read write };
#     allow appdomain zygote_tmpfs:file         { map read };
#     allow appdomain zygote:memfd_file         { map read };
#     allow appdomain hal_codec2:memfd_file     { getattr map read write };
#     allow appdomain cameraserver:memfd_file   { getattr map read write };
#     allow appdomain mediaextractor:memfd_file { getattr map read write };
#     allow appdomain mediaserver:memfd_file    { getattr map read write };
#
# The first three are granted in both forms, the last four only as memfd_file.
# That asymmetry is invisible on a kernel that implements the memfd_file
# security class and total on one that does not: mainline has no such class, so
# a memfd is an ordinary tmpfs file carrying the creating process's tmpfs type,
# every memfd_file rule silently matches nothing, and only the paired ones keep
# working. cameraserver was the first of the four to be noticed and is already
# handled in pc_cameraserver.te; this covers hal_codec2, and mediaserver and
# mediaextractor are covered in pc_mediaserver.te.
#
# So the whole family of denials on this device is one upstream assumption, not
# a series of unrelated faults, and the remedy is to supply the tmpfs half AOSP
# supplies for its own first three entries.
#
# Worth being deliberate about the breadth. The counterpart here has to be the
# generic tmpfs type, because hal_codec2 has no tmpfs type to name, so this is
# wider than the domain specific rules elsewhere: it reaches any tmpfs file
# whose creator lacks a type_transition, not only the codec's. It is bounded by
# being file class only, and it grants apps no more than the hal_codec2
# memfd_file rule already intends. A narrower rule would need mediaswcodec to
# declare a tmpfs type, which means changing platform policy rather than
# adding to it.
#
# MLS needs nothing here. private/mls exempts the read and write constraints
# when the object is app_data_file_type or appdomain_tmpfs, and for plain tmpfs
# at s0 a categorised app dominates s0 for read; write is the constraint that
# would bite, and tmpfs is not app data, so equivalence is not required of it.
allow appdomain tmpfs:file { getattr map read write };
